@startuml
title SIC/XE Simulator - Architecture Data Model
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0

class ArchitectureInfo {
    +name
    +description
    +memory : MemoryDescription
    +registers : RegisterDescription[]
    +instructions : InstructionDescription[]
}

class MemoryDescription {
    +size
    +address_width
    +word_size
    +alignment
    +endianness
}

class RegisterDescription {
    +id
    +name
    +width
    +description
}

class InstructionDescription {
    +id
    +mnemonic
    +min_size
    +max_size
    +opcode
    +flags
    +formats
    +operand_type
}

class Registers {
    -m_registers
    -m_id_ro_reg
    +allocate(id, name, initial_value)
    +deallocate(name)
    +get(name)
    +get_name(id)
}

class Memory {
    +read(address)
    +write(address, value)
}

class Word {
    <<24-bit value>>
}

class Float {
    <<48-bit SIC/XE floating value>>
    +operator double()
    +operator=(double)
}

ArchitectureInfo *-- MemoryDescription
ArchitectureInfo *-- RegisterDescription
ArchitectureInfo *-- InstructionDescription

Registers o-- Word : stores CPU values
Memory o-- Word : stores 24-bit words/bytes as model requires

note bottom
SIC/XE uses a 24-bit word and 48-bit floating-point format.
The simulator can expose these through native C++ representations
while preserving architectural bit width at the boundary.
end note

@enduml
